            { q: "What is the difference between strategic and tactical threat intelligence?", opts: ["No difference", "Strategic: high-level trends for executives; Tactical: technical IoCs for security tools", "Tactical is more important", "Strategic intelligence is automated"], a: 1, exp: "Strategic CTI informs business decisions (who targets us, why). Tactical CTI (hashes, IPs, domains) feeds directly into security tools." },
            { q: "What is STIX?", opts: ["A threat analysis tool", "Structured Threat Information eXpression — a standardized language for sharing cyber threat intelligence", "A type of malware", "A security certification"], a: 1, exp: "STIX provides a common language for threat intel. TAXII is the transport mechanism. Together they enable automated intel sharing between organizations." },
            { q: "What is a threat intelligence feed?", opts: ["A news feed about threats", "Continuously updated streams of threat data (IPs, domains, hashes) that security tools consume to detect threats", "Employee security training", "A vulnerability scanner feed"], a: 1, exp: "Threat feeds (AlienVault OTX, Recorded Future, CrowdStrike) provide real-time IoCs that SIEM, firewalls, and EDR tools use for detection." },
            { q: "What is the primary value of MITRE ATT&CK for detection engineering?", opts: ["It lists all CVEs", "It maps adversary techniques to detection opportunities — enabling systematic coverage assessment", "It provides attack tools", "It replaces incident response plans"], a: 1, exp: "ATT&CK enables purple teaming — mapping detections to techniques to identify blind spots and prioritize detection engineering efforts." }
