            { q: "How can AI reduce MTTR (Mean Time to Respond)?", opts: ["By replacing human analysts", "Automating triage, enrichment, and routine response actions, freeing humans for complex decisions", "By blocking all alerts", "By improving network speed"], a: 1, exp: "AI automates the first 80% of incident response (triage, enrichment, initial containment), dramatically reducing MTTR." },
            { q: "How can LLMs help with malware analysis?", opts: ["LLMs can remove malware automatically", "Explaining disassembled malware code in plain language, suggesting what the malware does and how to detect it", "LLMs write better malware", "They cannot help with malware"], a: 1, exp: "LLMs like ChatGPT and Claude can explain assembly code, deobfuscate scripts, and describe malware behavior — accelerating analysis for junior analysts." },
            { q: "What is AI-powered alert triage?", opts: ["AI closing all alerts automatically", "AI classifying alerts by severity, enriching them with context, and prioritizing for analyst review", "A faster SIEM", "Removing false positives permanently"], a: 1, exp: "AI triage evaluates each alert, looks up related intelligence, determines context, and prioritizes — analysts see pre-investigated, high-confidence findings." },
            { q: "What is the main risk of SOAR automation in IR?", opts: ["It's too expensive", "Automated actions can cause unintended consequences if playbooks are incorrect or edge cases aren't handled", "It doesn't work", "Automation is always safe"], a: 1, exp: "Automated containment (blocking IPs, isolating systems) can disrupt legitimate services if playbooks have errors. Human review for high-impact actions is essential." }
