            { q: "What does STRIDE stand for?", opts: ["Security Testing, Risk, Impact, Defense, Execution", "Spoofing, Tampering, Repudiation, Information Disclosure, Denial of Service, Elevation of Privilege", "A threat modeling tool", "Security controls framework"], a: 1, exp: "STRIDE categorizes threats by type. Each category maps to a specific security property that must be protected." },
            { q: "What is a trust boundary in threat modeling?", opts: ["A network firewall", "The line between components with different trust levels — where security controls must be applied", "A physical security perimeter", "An encryption boundary"], a: 1, exp: "Trust boundaries define where data crosses between different trust zones. All data crossing a trust boundary must be validated and controlled." },
            { q: "When should threat modeling occur in the SDLC?", opts: ["After deployment when issues are found", "During design — early enough to address architectural issues before they become expensive to fix", "During code review", "Only for high-risk applications"], a: 1, exp: "Threat modeling during design is 100x cheaper than fixing architectural issues after deployment. It shapes security requirements from the start." },
            { q: "What is an attack tree?", opts: ["A network diagram", "A hierarchical diagram decomposing an attack goal into specific attack paths and techniques", "A malware family tree", "An org chart for attackers"], a: 1, exp: "Attack trees break down how an attacker could achieve a goal. They help prioritize defenses by understanding which paths are most likely or impactful." }
